WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.280
What color is cyborg blood supposed to be?&nbsp;
How many bodies does Alita actually have?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.880
What exactly are those blue flames?&nbsp;
Keep watching for the “Alita:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.709
Battle Angel” details only real fans notice.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.720
One of the most memorable&nbsp;
and exciting parts of Alita:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.680
Battle Angel is the high-octane spectacle that&nbsp;
is motorball. There are two matches in the film:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.000
a skirmish on the streets of Iron City&nbsp;
and a professional tryout in a super-sized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.800
stadium. Despite these two sequences being&nbsp;
decidedly different in terms of the tone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.920
stakes, and even the visual presentation, both&nbsp;
of them utilize real-world camera physics.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.520
Even though the limitations of real-world&nbsp;
cameras don't apply when creating an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.080
action scene with CGI, director Robert&nbsp;
Rodriguez chose to self-impose those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.000
limitations to create a more grounded&nbsp;
sequence. In an interview with Inverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.880
he described his methodology for crafting this&nbsp;
scene with a sense of realism, as he revealed,

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.040
"I don't want the cameras flying around the&nbsp;
place, doing things impossible to shoot. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.320
wanted the physics of real cameras, placement you&nbsp;
would see in NASCAR. Long lenses, capturing things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.280
whizzing by, as well as cameras on the track with&nbsp;
the players to keep it as real world as possible."

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.360
Pulling inspiration from the shooting style of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.720
real-world sports and sticking only&nbsp;
to shots that would be possible with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.000
a physical camera ultimately grounded the&nbsp;
bombastic action with a sense of reality.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.560
The character of Alita starts out the film as&nbsp;
a total amnesiac. While struggling to recall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.880
details from her past, she finds herself&nbsp;
stumbling into some dangerous situations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.200
Luckily, she possesses an instinctive fighting&nbsp;
technique that kicks in when she's threatened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.560
This combat style is known as Panzer Kunst, a&nbsp;
German term which translates into English as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.600
"Armor Art." Panzer Kunst comes directly from the&nbsp;
manga series that inspired the film. Within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:45.920
series lore, Panzer Kunst originated on Mars as a&nbsp;
martial-arts style for use in zero-gravity combat.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.800
In real life, Panzer Kunst refers to the art&nbsp;
of defeating armored opponents in combat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.600
which is fitting considering that the majority of&nbsp;
combatants in Alita are heavily armored cyborgs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.080
The first descriptions of real-life Panzer&nbsp;
Kunst in sword fighting are believed to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:05.520
from a German fencing manual from the 1440s.&nbsp;
This instructional guide offered insights into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.440
fighting techniques to be used against&nbsp;
both armored and unarmored opponents in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.600
a variety of combat situations. Though the&nbsp;
manual was primarily focused on fencing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.720
there were also techniques described for other&nbsp;
weaponry and also unarmed combat. As Alita&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.200
typically dispatches her foes with a mixture of&nbsp;
martial-arts abilities and sword-fighting prowess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.720
her fighting style lines up well&nbsp;
with this 15th-century inspiration.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:27.280
Alita's favorite food

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.760
After being brought back into consciousness&nbsp;
for the first time in centuries by Dr. Ido,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.160
Alita is a blank slate. In her quest for&nbsp;
self-discovery, she seeks to learn not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.960
only who she is, but also what she believes and&nbsp;
what she likes and dislikes. When Hugo learns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:46.080
that Alita has never tasted chocolate before,&nbsp;
he sets about remedying that immediately. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:50.234
after trying it for the first time, Alita&nbsp;
declares that she now has a favorite food.

00:02:50.949 --> 00:02:54.960
"I have a favorite food now!&nbsp;
This is my favorite food."

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.480
It might look like a simple chocolate bar,&nbsp;
but it's actually a chocolate shell packed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.520
with a honey-peanut-butter filling and pecans. We&nbsp;
know this because it was made by Robert Rodriguez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.920
himself, and he even put together a tutorial&nbsp;
explaining how to make your own at home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.880
He used a 3D-printer to make a custom&nbsp;
chocolate mold in the shape of the Zalem symbol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.600
found in the world of Alita, and he even brought&nbsp;
homemade snacks to the cast of the film as well.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.320
Throughout his career, Rodriguez's love of food&nbsp;
has popped up in his work time and time again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.320
as evidenced by his long-running "10-Minute&nbsp;
Cooking School" series that often appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.280
in the DVD extras of his movies. This has&nbsp;
included making Texas BBQ for Planet Terror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.240
presenting his recipe for&nbsp;
breakfast tacos for Sin City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.626
and explaining how to cook puerco&nbsp;
pibil for Once Upon a Time in Mexico.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.920
Long before the Alita: Battle Angel film arrived&nbsp;
in theaters, the story was first told in manga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.960
form all the way back in 1990. The first run of&nbsp;
the manga series ran for nine volumes that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.520
released over the course of five years, and&nbsp;
multiple sequel series followed. In English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.800
this manga is known as Battle Angel Alita, though&nbsp;
the original Japanese title translates directly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.920
"Gun Dream." During an interview with BBC Radio&nbsp;
1, James Cameron described the switch from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:09.120
"Battle Angel Alita" to "Alita: Battle Angel"&nbsp;
as a forward-thinking shift with sequels in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:15.040
mind. He suggested such potential sequel titles as&nbsp;
"Alita: Fallen Angel" and "Alita: Avenging Angel."

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.640
In the manga and in the 1993 anime adaptation&nbsp;
"Battle Angel," the main character's name wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.760
Alita at all, but rather "Gally." The name was&nbsp;
changed for the initial English translation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.320
and it stuck around for the&nbsp;
big-screen version as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.800
There's another important difference in Alita's&nbsp;
namesake that changed between the manga and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.280
film. In the manga, Dr. Ido names Gally&nbsp;
after his cat who recently passed away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.626
while the live-action adaptation ups the ante and&nbsp;
has Dr. Ido name Alita after his dead daughter.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.280
There are a couple of moments in Alita:&nbsp;
Battle Angel when little blue flames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.040
emerge from Alita's body. Though never&nbsp;
explicitly explained in the film, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:56.160
play an important role later on in the plot and&nbsp;
serve an even more important purpose in the manga.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.760
In the manga lore, these little flames&nbsp;
are an extension of Alita's ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.160
manipulate plasma. What might look&nbsp;
like a flame is actually arc plasma.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:08.560
These plasma powers contribute to Alita's&nbsp;
fighting prowess by increasing her speed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.520
magnifying the power of her strikes. When she&nbsp;
throws a punch, it isn't just a synthetic fist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.080
striking her opponent, but also the&nbsp;
destructive power of plasma energy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:22.560
Toward the end of the film when she finally gets&nbsp;
her hands on the much sought-after Damascus Blade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:27.360
her arc plasma abilities come into play again.&nbsp;
You might notice that the blade turns blue and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.200
glows in her hand, something it never&nbsp;
did while in Zapan's possession. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.604
is due to her arc plasma powers surging&nbsp;
through the blade and supercharging it.

00:05:36.477 --> 00:05:38.477
"Didn't exactly come with a manual, did you?"

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:44.720
Alita: Battle Angel was released in the United&nbsp;
States on February 14, 2019, but James Cameron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:49.520
bought the film rights to the manga all the way&nbsp;
back in 2000. He even expressed interest in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.880
day adapting the story as early as 1995, before&nbsp;
the release of Titanic. He was ultimately able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.920
to buy the adaptation rights directly from the&nbsp;
manga's creator for the sum of one million yen.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:04.080
Cameron originally planned on directing the&nbsp;
film himself, and he considered going into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.040
production on Alita instead of 2009's Avatar.&nbsp;
Of course, that didn't end up happening. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.800
key reason for holding off on Alita was&nbsp;
that he didn't think CGI technology was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.880
quite ready to deliver what was needed to&nbsp;
do it properly. After the massive financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.600
success and major technological innovations&nbsp;
of Avatar, Cameron again toyed around with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.080
idea of directing Alita, but his attachments&nbsp;
to the Avatar sequels made that impossible.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.720
Enter Robert Rodriguez, a friend of Cameron's&nbsp;
for many years who had seen materials from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.200
Cameron's earlier false-starts with Alita.&nbsp;
He asked if the adaptation would ever happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.280
if Cameron was going to be busy with the&nbsp;
Avatar franchise for the rest of his career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:44.800
So Cameron then offered Rodriguez the reins.&nbsp;
As Rodriguez told Fandango All Access,

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.320
"Cause we'd been looking for things&nbsp;
to work with, on together for years."

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.408
And this was a perfect&nbsp;
opportunity for a collaboration.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.320
The blue blood of the cyborgs in Alita might&nbsp;
feel reminiscent of other sci-fi properties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.320
such as the inky-blue blood of the&nbsp;
androids in 2013's The World's End.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:05.760
But the blood in Alita wasn't always blue.&nbsp;
In the 1993 anime adaptation Battle Angel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:11.280
the cyborg blood was instead a bright crimson. And&nbsp;
although the manga was inked in black and white,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.600
there was a clear emphasis on gore&nbsp;
that didn't make it into the 2019 film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.160
In an interview with BBC Radio 1, James Cameron&nbsp;
revealed that he couldn't be completely faithful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.720
to the manga because of how gory it was,&nbsp;
so they softened it for a PG-13 rating.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:29.120
Even with this softening, Alita pushes&nbsp;
the limits of PG-13, as there's plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:35.040
of biomechanical destruction and dismemberment.&nbsp;
The MPAA's ratings board has historically been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.880
more lenient when assigning ratings to&nbsp;
films with unnaturally colored blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.480
so tinting the cyborg blood blue&nbsp;
helped to avoid an R rating.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.280
This isn't the first film directed by Robert&nbsp;
Rodriguez to use unnaturally colored blood in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:53.200
a bid to procure a more audience-friendly rating,&nbsp;
as 1996's From Dusk Till Dawn featured green blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:58.800
for the vampires instead of red. That decision&nbsp;
was made out of a fear of receiving an NC-17,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.880
which worked out in the film's favor,&nbsp;
as it ultimately secured an R rating.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:08.000
Alita gets her first taste of motorball while&nbsp;
playing in a friendly neighborhood skirmish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:12.000
In contrast to the later stadium motorball&nbsp;
match, this first game was intended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.040
to be executed with little-to-no CGI outside of&nbsp;
the title character. This requirement incurred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:21.280
a number of additional challenges, the first of&nbsp;
which was finding the right talent to fill the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:26.400
roles of the other players, who had to play this&nbsp;
larger-than-life sport as if it was second nature.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.480
So stunt coordinator Garrett Warren brought&nbsp;
together the world's top professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:35.840
in-line skaters to execute the scene. In a&nbsp;
making-of video, Warren, producer Jon Landau,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:41.280
and members of the cast and stunt team discuss how&nbsp;
the unique challenges of the scene were overcome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:46.000
Tall drop-in ramps and mechanical winches&nbsp;
were used to mimic the speed and momentum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.400
achieved by the motorized rollerblades of&nbsp;
the film, which don't exist in real life.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.360
The special effects of Alita: Battle Angel&nbsp;
exist at the cutting edge of what's possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:00.080
with modern filmmaking technology. Having a&nbsp;
large budget wasn't all that it took to pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:05.360
off the impressive visuals, as it also required&nbsp;
the best design team and technology available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.160
Rather than scouring the industry&nbsp;
for the best in the business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:11.600
the necessary resources came&nbsp;
from a little closer to home.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:16.320
James Cameron had already assembled the perfect&nbsp;
design team to work on the Avatar sequels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.080
So when Robert Rodriguez got started&nbsp;
on Alita, Cameron lent him the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:24.240
for a few months to work out the look,&nbsp;
feel, and creation of the characters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.720
world. The production also made use of the&nbsp;
motion-capture and 3D camera technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.840
previously pioneered by Cameron for both the&nbsp;
Avatar sequels and his documentary projects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:38.160
Cameron wanted a realistic "nuts and&nbsp;
bolts" look to the cyborgs and the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.640
while Rodriguez pulled from his experiences&nbsp;
as a cartoonist to bring a lighter sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.440
style to the film's look and feel. Ultimately,&nbsp;
a happy medium was found with this design team.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:53.600
The only human part of Alita's physiology is her&nbsp;
brain, while the rest of her body is synthetic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.760
Even her heart is a pricey, centuries-old&nbsp;
relic. This means that her body can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:02.000
swapped out with different cyborg parts&nbsp;
tailored to specific tasks. In the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:07.120
we see her in two main cyborg bodies. One is a&nbsp;
civilian body initially built in part for Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:12.480
Ido's daughter before her death, while the other&nbsp;
is a reclaimed berserker body built for combat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.960
There are also a few quick glimpses&nbsp;
at another cyborg body in flashbacks.

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:20.240
"A warrior's spirit needs a warrior's body."

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:26.240
However, in the manga and anime, Alita has&nbsp;
even more cyborg bodies that she can switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:30.240
between that are best suited to particular&nbsp;
activities. Before the motorball match in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.960
the film, Dr. Ido insists that Alita wear&nbsp;
protective pads on top of her Berserker body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:39.360
but in the manga lore, Alita has a&nbsp;
specialized body made just for motorball.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:42.960
This motorball body has less destructive&nbsp;
capabilities than her berserker body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:47.680
but it's much faster and more flexible. If Alita:&nbsp;
Battle Angel ever winds up getting a sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:59.840
there's a chance we might see more&nbsp;
of her specialized cyborg bodies.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:01.040
